			<div class="content" id="post-content-2127467">
									

<p>Thanks!</p>
<p>I agree about AA. I think it ultimately served to develop the climax very well, but it was a relatively minor objection I had to check-box along the way when getting into the world-building.</p>
<p>It was similar to my feelings on his frequent, mostly out-of-context Lovecraftian name-dropping in the first book. That normally turns me off in modern horror, but he explained in the afterward (I think) that he wasn't aware of the Call of Cthulhu rpg and the resurgence of Lovecraft, so he thought he was being clever.</p>
<p>I think it was much better done in the Fuller Memorandum, although that might just be because it was a more obscure historical figure and a much more plot-centric final result.</p>
<div class="collapsible-block">
<div class="collapsible-block-folded"><a class="collapsible-block-link" href="javascript:;">+&nbsp;show&nbsp;block</a></div>
